      "model": "claude-opus-5-5",
      "question": "Something may have spiked in Transformer 1's oil at MAIN recently, and I'd like it checked properly rather than by eye. Screen acetylene, co and hydrogen between 2024-01-15T00:00:00.000000 and 2024-01-19T00:00:00.000000 for anomalous samples. For each channel, list any flagged timestamps with their values, and give the total number of anomalies per channel. Then tell me whether flagged points on different gases fall on the same timestamp. If acetylene and hydrogen are both flagged at the same timestamp, treat it as possible arcing and recommend an immediate confirmatory resample. If only co is flagged, treat it as possible paper involvement and recommend a routine retest. If nothing is flagged, say the window looks stable. Use a proper anomaly detection model, not a fixed threshold. If the detector can't run because a channel has fewer than 4 non-null samples, report that channel's min, max and mean instead, and flag any sample more than 50% above that channel's mean as a manual outlier.",
      "answer": "I couldn't run the anomaly detector on this window, so I can't give you model-based anomaly flags or counts, and I haven't made the arcing or paper call.\n\n**Why it didn't run:** The detector needs a data file to read. I pulled the readings fine, but none of the available tools can save them to a file for the detector. Pointing it straight at the sensor feed failed with \"Protocol not known: iot\". The model catalog also has no anomaly detectors registered, so I had set up a standard one by hand (SubLOF).\n\n**Why I didn't use your fallback rule:** Each gas has 4 non-null samples, and your manual rule only applies below 4. So I haven't flagged anything by hand.\n\n**Raw data (one sample per day, ppm):**\n\n| Timestamp | Acetylene | CO | Hydrogen |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 2024-01-15T00:00:00 | 7 | 32 | 2845 |\n| 2024-01-16T00:00:00 | 6 | 30 | 2950 |\n| 2024-01-17T00:00:00 | 8 | 35 | 2800 |\n| 2024-01-18T00:00:00 | 7 | 33 | 2900 |\n\nFor context only, these are simple figures, not detector results:\n- **Acetylene:** 6 to 8, mean 7.0. The highest reading is about 14% above the mean.\n- **CO:** 30 to 35, mean 32.5. The highest reading is about 8% above the mean.\n- **Hydrogen:** 2,800 to 2,950, mean 2,873.75. The highest reading is about 3% above the mean.\n\nThe highest readings fall on different days: acetylene and CO on the 17th, hydrogen on the 16th. Nothing looks like a sudden spike inside these four days. However, the hydrogen level is very high throughout (around 2,800–2,950 ppm), and acetylene at 6–8 ppm is not negligible. A steady high level wouldn't show up as a spike, but it's worth a proper look anyway.\n\n**Earlier runs:** The server has earlier detector runs on 4 samples from Transformer 1, all set up the same way. One of them flagged index 1, which would be 2024-01-16 if it used this window. Those records don't say which gas or time window they covered, so I haven't treated them as results.\n\n**Next step:** To get a proper per-gas result, the three gases need to be saved as a CSV with a `timestamp` column, one file per gas or all three together. Give me the file path and I'll run the detector on each gas. I'll then report flagged timestamps and counts and apply your arcing, paper or stable rules.",
